Marionette AirDrop driver candidate 0040 -- selectable carrier vdev (first probe of the
P2P/GO-carrier direction).

Applies on top of 0039 (native/airdrop/driver/0039-monitor-inject-tx-spec.patch).
File: qcacld-3.0/core/hdd/src/wlan_hdd_tx_rx.c

Why
---
0037, 0038 and 0039 drove the very same 24 well-formed frames down three
different host paths and got the very same firmware assert three times:

    mhi mhi_1107_00.01.00: [E][mhi_process_sfr] cmnos_thread_patch.c:1170:0x8
    Asserted in:0x18dc754:0x1801164, line#2834

The assert PC did not move between a plain vdev-based TCL MSDU, an
exception MSDU with an all-zero HTT ext2 header, and an exception MSDU whose
HTT ext2 header carries a complete transmit specification (host_tx_desc_pool,
power, mcs, nss, preamble, retries, bandwidth).  That rules out every
"the host did not tell the firmware enough" hypothesis and leaves one
question open: is the refusal about the frame, or about the vdev it is
addressed to?

The monitor vdev is the odd one out.  It has no rate control, no peer, and
no TX descriptor pool of its own -- 0037 had to create that pool by hand
because the firmware's flow-pool-map never reaches a monitor vdev.  Every
other vdev on the target (station, SAP, P2P GO, P2P client) transmits data
frames continuously, so the firmware demonstrably knows how to build a PPDU
there.

What
----
A module parameter selects the vdev that carries an injected frame:

    marionette_awdl_raw_tx_vdev = -1 (default)  monitor adapter's own vdev
                                0..N            that vdev instead

-1 preserves the behaviour of 0036 to 0039 byte for byte, so the control
case is unchanged.  A vdev id of 0 or more routes the identical frame,
identical metadata and identical queue to a vdev the firmware already
transmits on.  The station is the cheapest probe: it already exists, already
owns a flow pool, and sits on the channel the monitor was tuned to.

Known cost, measured rather than assumed: the frame is built for AWDL, so
addr3 is the AWDL BSSID rather than the access point's BSSID and addr1 is the
peer rather than the AP.  Whether the firmware drops, rewrites or passes such
a frame through on a station vdev is exactly part of what this measures, and
the access point may react to it.  The harness still watches the station
association and the gateway round trip, and the rollback guardian still
restores the stock stack.

Test harness
------------
volatile-stack-test.sh gains the action run-rawtx-sta-vdev, which is
run-rawtx with marionette_awdl_raw_tx_vdev=0.  The summary prints which vdev
carried the frames so a log is never ambiguous about it.

